=== docs/concepts/the-dual-write-trap.md <-> website/src/pages/learn/the-dual-write-trap.mdx: 11 only-in-docs, 13 only-in-website
  D: One system decides whether the change happened, and one transaction commits it.
  D: See also - The outbox - the table and the write path - Actions - the two-phase lifecycle that makes it automatic - Sagas - coordinating multi-step work without pretending it is one transaction
  D: The dual-write trap Every business change produces two things at once - a new state, and a record of how it got there.
  D: The one place the trap reappears A cross-shard action is conceptually a dual write again - two databases, two commits, no atomicity between them.
  D: The rest of Concepts is how the pieces make that structural rather than a convention somebody has to police.
  D: This is the dual-write problem , and it is the most common source of "the events do not match the data" in event-driven systems.
  D: Why it's a framework concern The pattern is well known, and most teams bolt it on:
  D: Your code stages a DepositMoneyAction ;
  D: app ↓ ┌─────────────────────────┐ │ database (one tx) │ │ ┌──────┐ ┌────────┐ │ │ │state │ │ outbox │ │ │ └──────┘ └────────┘ │ └─────────────────────────┘ ↓ Kafka (Debezium, or a poller) The outbox is an ordinary table in your application database.
  D: app ↙ ↘ DB Kafka The bug is the gap between them.
  D: the executor opens the transaction, writes the wallet row, writes the matching MoneyDepositedEvent , commits.
